usb: typec: tcpm: the UMP9620's Type-C and PD blocks as a TCPM port controller (ZTE U30 Air, experimental)

A PD dock with a charger behind it (USB-C charger in, USB-A ports and Ethernet out) should charge the U30 Air and
have it as the USB host at once. Mainline had no driver for the PMIC's Type-C and PD blocks: VBUS from a GPIO,
the source's Rp read by the charger (0012), and the data role chosen by mu300-usb. kano ported Unisoc's
sc27xx_pd.c to mainline's TCPM (kanoqwq/mu300-linux 2af101f8, "complete U30 Air power, LED and PD dock support");
on his unit a computer gave device + sink, and a dock with its charger gave host + sink after a manual data role
swap, while the automatic switch to host did not, and his last receive fix was not run against the dock again.

This is his driver reviewed and rewritten (FINDINGS 150), one file, Unisoc's register sequences checked against
sc27xx_pd.c and sc27xx_typec.c of ZTE's U30 Air kernel. Built as a module, and probe does nothing unless the
module is loaded with enable=1 (mu300-usb pd on); then only where the device tree has the charger policy service
(the U30 Air, as 0016), the Type-C block's use_pdhub_c2c and the PD block enabled (the F50's is disabled, the V50
has no charger policy service).

What changed from kano's version, and why:

- VCONN is never sourced, and a cable's Ra is not reported: ZTE's firmware does not source it (the PD node has no
  vconn-supply, so Unisoc's set_vconn fails), and which CC pin the PMIC's VCONN switch drives is unknown; kano
  switched the switch on whenever TCPM asked.
- 5 V out only through the charger's regulator, which refuses an outside supply (0019); a missing vbus-supply is an
  error, not a dummy regulator (devm_regulator_get gave one).
- The Type-C block's mode and enable registers are saved at probe and put back at remove and shutdown: the PMIC
  keeps its registers across a reboot, and a block left source-only would not let a charger see a sink (LK, Android,
  the next boot). The PD clocks are never switched off again (the module enable covers the Type-C block LK and
  Android rely on); kano switched them off at shutdown.
- Clocks and calibration in init, in Unisoc's order (kano calibrated the Type-C trim before the clocks were on).
- A new PD session (set_pd_rx on) and every hard reset, sent or received, empty both FIFOs and clear both message
  IDs, as Unisoc does; kano cleared neither the RX ID at a new session nor the TX ID at a hard reset, so a first
  message could be taken for a repeat.
- The input current is passed on only for an explicit contract (the partner sent Source_Capabilities); kano passed
  it on after any received message, Rp-based values included.
- Unplugged as a host, the data role goes back to device at once: TCPM never hands it back at a detach.
- The receive-error interrupt is not enabled (nothing is done with it; Unisoc has a storm guard for it).
- Locking: every callback that touches the PD block holds the driver's lock; TCPM is called with it held only where
  TCPM merely queues (receive, transmit complete, hard reset).
- Source PDO 5 V 400 mA (ZTE's own, from the board's connector node), sink 5 V 2 A, Rp default as a source.

Kept from kano's version: the Type-C block's own state machine for attach, detach and the CC pin (open is not
done: TCPM's port reset at start then does not drop a computer that is plugged in), the FIFO flushed after each
frame (it holds the CRC behind it), TX_OK and a received GoodCRC completing one transmission once, the VBUS GPIO's
extcon for VBUS changes without a CC event, TCPM registered last and unregistered first.

Not known yet and to be seen on the device (FINDINGS 150): whether the Type-C block follows a mode change while
attached (power role swaps, Try.SNK), whether a hard reset sent raises TX_OK, and whether a second frame can sit in
the FIFO behind the first (the RX FIFO level is logged at debug level).

--- a/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/Kconfig
@@ -78,6 +78,19 @@
 	  To compile this driver as module, choose M here: the module will be
 	  called typec_wcove.ko
 
+config TYPEC_UMP9620
+	tristate "Unisoc UMP9620 Type-C and PD port controller (ZTE U30 Air)"
+	depends on MFD_SC27XX_PMIC && OF && REGULATOR && NVMEM && EXTCON
+	depends on USB_ROLE_SWITCH && POWER_SUPPLY
+	help
+	  The USB-C port of the UMP9620 PMIC under TCPM: Type-C attach and
+	  detach from the PMIC's own Type-C block, USB Power Delivery through
+	  its PD block, the dwc3 data role and the charger's boost as VBUS.
+	  Experimental: it does nothing unless loaded with enable=1.
+
+	  To compile this driver as a module, choose M here: the module will be
+	  called ump9620-tcpc.
+
